        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τὴν ἁμαρτίαν εἰς ἐκεῖνον ἀναφερομένην ἔχει, οὗπερ καθ᾽ ὁμοίωσιν ἡ εἰκὼν ἐτύγχανεν οὖσα.",
          "english_quote": "has the sin charged to the one whose likeness the image happened to bear.",
          "issue": "“Charged to” makes the represented person appear to bear the offender’s guilt. In this argument, εἰς ἐκεῖνον ἀναφερομένην describes the offense as reaching or being directed against the person represented by the insulted image, not as transferring culpability to that person.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use wording that preserves the direction of the offense, such as “commits a sin against the one whose likeness the image happened to bear.”",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
